      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(acp): ACP session startup fails when backend does not advertise thinking config",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes #103802 — Every `sessions_spawn(runtime: \"acp\")` against backends like OpenCode (which only advertise `mode, model` as supported config options) fails immediately with:\n\n```\nAcpRuntimeError: ACP backend \"acpx\" does not accept config key \"thinking\".: code=ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL\n```\n\nThe ACP runtime resolves a thinking default (e.g., `\"off\"` for non-reasoning provider models) and sends it via `setConfigOption`, which the backend rejects. The caller cannot avoid this — passing no `thinking` in `sessions_spawn` does not prevent the injection, and omitting `agents.defaults.thinkingDefault` doesn't either.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nTwo layers of defense, following the precedent of PR #81603 (timeout tolerance):\n\n**Layer 1 — Prevention (runtime-options.ts):** A `shouldEmitThinkingConfigOption` guard in `buildRuntimeConfigOptionPairs`. If the backend advertises `configOptionKeys` and none of the thinking aliases (`thinking`, `effort`, `reasoning_effort`, `thought_level`) are in the list, the thinking pair is never emitted. Follows the exact same pattern as the existing `shouldEmitTimeoutConfigOption` guard.\n\n**Layer 2 — Safety net (manager.runtime-controls.ts):** A catch block in `applyManagerRuntimeControls` that silently skips unsupported thinking config options when `setConfigOption` throws. Error detection is restricted to `ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL` error code only — value-validation errors (e.g., `-32602`, `invalid params`) are NOT swallowed, so explicit user-selected values that a backend rejects still fail visibly.\n\n## User Impact\n\nACP spawns targeting backends with limited config support (mode+model only) succeed instead of failing on init. Backends that support thinking/effort continue to work as before — Layer 1 only suppresses emission when the backend explicitly does not advertise the key, and Layer 2 only catches actual unsupported-control rejections.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- 4 files changed, 161 insertions, 1 deletion (2 source + 2 test)\n- Follows the existing `shouldEmitTimeoutConfigOption` / `isUnsupportedOptionalTimeoutConfigRejection` patterns exactly\n- **15 new tests** across both test files:\n  - `runtime-options.test.ts`: 7 tests for thinking advertisement filtering (omitted when unadvertised, emitted for each alias, emitted when unknown, doesn't affect other keys)\n  - `manager.runtime-config.test.ts`: 2 tests for thinking rejection handling (unsupported control silently skipped, value errors still propagate)\n- All 36 tests pass, `oxlint` reports 0 errors, 0 warnings\n- Validated locally: the same logic was applied as a compiled JS patch to a running OpenClaw instance targeting an OpenCode ACP backend. Before the patch, every ACP spawn failed with `ACP_BACKEND_UNSUPPORTED_CONTROL`. After the patch, the thinking config option is silently skipped and session startup completes normally.\n",
        "branch": "fix/acp-opencode-thinking-config",
